---
import Base from '../../../layouts/Base.astro';
import SiteBar from '../../../components/SiteBar.astro';
import SiteFooter from '../../../components/SiteFooter.astro';
// How the rig measures: four chapters over five animated pages (public/how-it-works/*.html), each
// page made for the review to show one part of the method. They are self-contained (three.js from
// /vendor/three/), with their captions in Korean; the text here walks through each one in English.
// Embedded with ?embed=1 they show only the drawing, one thin progress bar and the caption, play on
// their own, loop, and pause while off screen or behind a tab. ?bg= is the section colour. A chapter
// is one grid: the animation on the left, the copy and what to look for beside it. The lens chapter
// has two pages behind tabs; the stacks are run by src/lib/viewer-tabs.js, and a page loads only
// when its stack comes near the screen.
const steps = [
  {
    id: 'shutter', n: 1, title: 'Global shutter', sub: 'Rolling shutter against global shutter', ratio: 'tall',
    pages: [{ id: 'shutter', page: 'shutter.html', title: 'Global shutter' }],
    text: [
      'A rolling shutter exposes the sensor one row at a time, from the top row down. While the exposure line travels down the frame the blade keeps turning, so each row shows the blade at a different moment and a straight blade comes out bent. A fast bar or a knee does the same thing.',
      'A global shutter exposes every row at the same instant. The frame is one moment. The three cameras on the rig fire on one trigger within 0.12 ms, so the three images are three views of the same instant.',
    ],
    look: ['The line across the sensor divides the rows already exposed from those still moving.', 'The chart under each sensor is the exposure time of every row.'],
  },
  {
    id: 'lens', n: 2, title: 'Lens calibration', sub: 'Focal length, optical centre and distortion, per camera', ratio: 'wide',
    pages: [
      { id: 'lens', tab: 'The principle', page: 'lens-principle.html', title: 'Lens calibration, the principle' },
      { id: 'lens-steps', tab: 'As the rig runs it', page: 'lens-steps.html', title: 'Lens calibration, as the rig runs it' },
    ],
    text: [
      'Before a pixel can be turned into a direction in space, the camera has to be described: where the optical centre sits, how long the focal length is and how the lens bends straight lines towards the edges. These numbers are the intrinsics, the matrix K and the distortion terms, and they are different for each of the three cameras.',
      'The camera stays where it is and a calibration board is shown to it in about ten poses. The corners found in every shot pair a point on the board with a pixel, and from those pairs K and the distortion terms are solved. The result is one K file per camera that every later step reads.',
    ],
    look: ['The small window at the top right is what the camera sees.', 'As the rig runs it has six stages; the K file is written only after shots held out of the fit pass the check.'],
  },
  {
    id: 'position', n: 3, title: 'Position calibration', sub: 'Position and orientation of the three cameras, relative to the rack', ratio: 'wide',
    pages: [{ id: 'position', page: 'ext-principle.html', title: 'Position calibration' }],
    text: [
      'Lens calibration describes each camera on its own; position calibration places the three in one frame of reference, the rack. A board is set at a known position on the rack and each camera observes it, and from the board’s corners each camera’s rotation and position are solved relative to the rack. The result is checked against the rack’s real dimensions and written to a file.',
      'From then on every point the cameras see gets a position in centimetres from the rack’s origin. Because the cameras are fixed to the rack, the calibration carries over to the next session.',
    ],
    look: ['Four stages: floor, rack origin, camera to camera, file.', 'The rack is the PVC mock-up at its real dimensions.', 'What moves into view is what was computed.'],
  },
  {
    id: 'measure', n: 4, title: 'Measurement', sub: 'From three images of one instant to joints, metrics and load', ratio: 'wide',
    pages: [{ id: 'measure', page: 'measure-principle.html', title: 'Measurement' }],
    text: [
      'For every triggered frame the joint detector finds the 2D joints in each of the three images. With K the lens distortion is taken out of those pixels, and each pixel becomes a ray leaving its camera. The rays for one joint from the three cameras meet at one point in space; that intersection is the joint’s 3D position. All 33 joints are found this way, frame after frame through the set.',
      'Thanks to the position calibration those points are in rack coordinates, so depth, lean, left-right asymmetry and the path of the bar are read directly. From the change in joint positions and the load on the bar, the load on each joint is computed per rep, and the reps are counted from the motion.',
    ],
    look: ['Four stages: 2D joints, rays, intersection, rack coordinates and metrics.', 'Camera 1 is mounted upright, so its small window is upright too.', 'The closing burst is the same computation repeated frame after frame.'],
  },
];
---
<Base
  lang="en"
  title="How it works — RackTracker"
  description="How RackTracker measures a set: why the cameras use a global shutter, how each lens is calibrated, how the three cameras are placed in the rack's coordinates, and how three images of one instant become 33 joints, metrics and the load on each joint. Five interactive animations."
  ogType="article"
  ogTitle="How RackTracker works"
  ogDescription="Global shutter, lens calibration, position calibration and measurement, in five interactive animations."
  path="/en/how-it-works/"
  alt="/how-it-works/"
>
  <SiteBar lang="en" current="/en/how-it-works/" alt="/how-it-works/" />

  <main id="top">
    <section class="wing first" id="overview" aria-labelledby="hiw-title">
      <div class="head">
        <h2 id="hiw-title">How it works</h2>
        <p>In the order the rig measures a set</p>
      </div>
      <div class="text">
        <p class="lede">The rig turns three photographs of one instant into the 3D position of 33 joints, and from those into metrics and the load on each joint. The captions are in Korean; the text beside each animation tells the same in English. The 3D scenes can be turned by dragging.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <nav class="steps-nav" aria-label="Chapters">
      <ol>{steps.map((s) => <li><a href={`#${s.id}`}><span class="num">{s.n}</span>{s.title}</a></li>)}</ol>
    </nav>

    {steps.map((s) => (
      <section class="wing hiw" id={s.id} aria-labelledby={`${s.id}-title`}>
        <div class="head">
          <h2 id={`${s.id}-title`}><span class="num">{s.n}</span>{s.title}</h2>
          <p>{s.sub}</p>
        </div>
        {s.pages.slice(1).map((p) => <span id={p.id}></span>)}
        <div class="hiw-grid">
          <div class="hiw-stage">
            {s.pages.length > 1 && (
              <div class="hw-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label={s.title} data-page={s.pages[0].id}>
                {s.pages.map((p, i) => <button type="button" role="tab" id={`tab-${p.id}`} aria-selected={i === 0 ? 'true' : 'false'} data-page={p.id}>{p.tab}</button>)}
              </div>
            )}
            <div class:list={['hw-frames', s.ratio]}>
              {s.pages.map((p, i) => (
                <div class:list={['hw-frame', 'hiw-frame', { on: i === 0 }]} data-page={p.id} id={`frame-${p.id}`} aria-hidden={i === 0 ? 'false' : 'true'} inert={i !== 0}>
                  <p class="placeholder">{p.title}</p>
                  <iframe title={p.title} data-src={`/how-it-works/${p.page}?embed=1&bg=eceef1`} loading="lazy"></iframe>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="hiw-text">
            {s.text.map((t) => <p class="lede">{t}</p>)}
            <dl class="facts look">
              <div><dt>What to look for</dt><dd><ul>{s.look.map((l) => <li>{l}</li>)}</ul></dd></div>
            </dl>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>
    ))}
  </main>

  <SiteFooter lang="en" />

  <script>
    import { mountViewerTabs } from '../../../lib/viewer-tabs.js';
    // one stack per chapter; a page loads once its stack comes near the screen
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('.hiw-stage').forEach((s) => mountViewerTabs(s, { margin: '400px 0px' }));
  </script>
</Base>
